// **The five things somebody should know before they commit to this race**, in one place
// because both pages that need them need exactly these five.
//
// `/nn/` shows them as *what this race is*, above the interest form. `/nn/2026/` shows them as
// *what you are about to enter*, above the entry form. Two copies of five safety-relevant
// bullets is two copies that drift, and the one that would drift is the one nobody re-read —
// so there is one copy, here.
//
// **The climb is the new one, and it was reaching nobody who was about to enter.** It was on
// `/nn/course/` and nowhere else, so `/nn/2026/` — the page with the entry form on it — told
// somebody what the ground is like and what to put on their feet, and nothing about the shape
// of the thing they were paying to run. Nightingale Valley is the part of this race people
// remember; it belongs with the other four rather than one tap away.
//
// **It is second rather than last**, because the first bullet is what is underfoot and this is
// the shape that ground is in. The two are one thought, and the order of the list is course
// facts, then the rule, then what is on the route, then where it starts.
//
// **"It is Bristol, in November. Plan for wet." joined the first bullet for the same reason.**
// It is the sentence that makes the trail-shoe line advice rather than trivia, and it was on
// the course page alone.
//
// **Both arrived word for word, and that is what makes the guard assertable.** While
// `course.astro` still exists the same sentences are on three pages, and `site.spec.ts`
// asserts them on all three at once — the shape it already uses for the water station. A
// tidier paraphrase here would have made that impossible and left three pages to drift
// instead of one component to maintain.
//
// **The water station says where it is now**, confirmed by the race director: approximately
// halfway. It was "one water station on the route" with no location, which is the fact somebody
// rations a bottle against — and this component is the only place it reaches both `/nn/` and
// `/nn/2026/`. `course.astro` carries the same sentence and got the same words, so the two
// cannot drift while that page still exists.
//
// **The headphone rule is not decoration.** ARC's rules contain no headphone provision, but
// Rule 81 lets an organiser make additional rules binding on competitors as though they were
// ARC's own — so this is the club's rule and it is enforceable, on a course Rule 83(3) shares
// with the public. It does not come off a page in a copy edit.
//
// ## What is deliberately not in it
//
// **A time.** The bullet used to end "everybody walks down together at 10:30", and 10:30 is a
// row in this year's schedule rather than a property of the race. It is on
// `/nn/<year>/race-day/`, in the schedule the committee edits, and this says the part that is
// true whichever year it is: you do not have to find the start on your own.
//
// **A distance from HQ to the start.** Nobody has supplied one, and deriving it from the
// coordinates and a guessed school entrance is exactly the plausible-looking number this
// repository keeps out of markup. See the note in `2026/race-day.astro`.
//
// **Where the route goes**, and this one is a near miss worth writing down. It reaches
// `/nn/2026/` in the same commit these bullets did, but as a paragraph on that page rather
// than through here — because `/nn/` already says it in the race director's own words, and a
// component that also said it would put two spellings of one route on that page. The climb
// above is the exception that proves it: the *shape* of the course is what somebody decides
// on, and it is stated once. See the note in `2026/index.astro`.
//
// Every line here is the club's own 2023 copy and is **draft pending committee approval**,
// like the rest of these pages.
---

<ul class="nn-arrows">
  {
    /* **`{' '}` is a real space, and it is here because a line break is not.** Prettier keeps
    the `<strong>` on a line of its own, `compressHTML` removes the newline, and this rendered
    "slippery when wet.Trail shoes recommended" on both pages that use this component from the
    day it was extracted. The explicit space survives the reformat; a typed one does not. The
    same trap is documented at the head of `nn/privacy.astro` and in CLAUDE.md. */
  }
  <li>
    A tough, properly off-road course — uneven, rocky, rooty, and slippery when wet. It is
    Bristol, in November. Plan for wet.{' '}
    <strong>Trail shoes recommended</strong>, road shoes acceptable.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Nightingale Valley is the climb.</strong> It is the part people remember, and the
    part that decides how the rest of your race feels. There is no clever way to run it — go
    up steadily, and enjoy how much better everything looks from the top.
  </li>
  <li><strong>No headphones of any type during the race.</strong></li>
  <li>
    One water station on the route at approximately halfway, one at the finish, and
    marshals throughout.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The start and the finish are not at race HQ.</strong> Everybody walks down together
    beforehand, so there is nothing to find on your own.
  </li>
</ul>
